## 5. Directional Vector Control & Energetic Polarity Assessment

This section evaluates how intentional directional forces (downward, upward, lateral) and applied energy polarities (negative, compressive, grounded vs. expansive) influence visual acuity, character resolution, and material perception during active reading.

### 5.1 Theoretical Framework & Energy Hypotheses
* **Downward / Negative Vector Control ($V_{\downarrow}^{-}$):** Application of downward force, downward gaze angle, or negative/grounding energy polarity to test if compressive focus enhances contrast, sharpens edge boundary detection, or causes visual interference/fatigue.
* **Upward / Positive Vector Control ($V_{\uparrow}^{+}$):** Application of upward force or positive/expansive orientation as a baseline control to evaluate relative changes in legibility, perceived font spec size, and processing speed.
* **Eye Tracker & Optometric Analogue:** Adapting standard visual acuity protocols (e.g., LogMAR chart, Landolt C orientation, optotype scaling) to record threshold limits under contrasting energetic conditions.

